package teyru

/* The zone database: ZoneId, ZoneOffset, ZoneRules, ZoneOffsetTransition and
   ZonedDateTime, over the operating system's own TZif files.

   java.time's zone layer has one source of truth, the IANA time zone database,
   and there are two ways to get at it. A JVM ships a compiled copy of the data
   in java.home/lib/tzdb.dat. A C program does not: it asks the C library, which
   asks the files under /usr/share/zoneinfo, which the distribution built from
   the same database with zic. This file reads those files, in Teyru, with no
   native code and no new C -- the format is a binary one (RFC 8536) and this
   library can read a file into a byte[], which is all a reader needs.

   What it depends on, exactly. The zoneinfo files of the host, in TZif format,
   under $TZDIR (in the sense the C library means: the variable, when it is set
   and not empty, is the directory the zone files are looked up in) and
   /usr/share/zoneinfo otherwise. There is no fallback database: a host with no
   tzdata (Windows, or a container that did not install it) has no zones, and
   every operation that needs one raises ZoneRulesException with a message that
   says which directory was not there. The two offsets that cannot be missing --
   UTC, GMT, UT, and the numeric ids like +08:00 -- are built in and work with
   no database at all, because a program that only needs UTC should not need
   tzdata. Everything else is refused by name rather than answered wrongly; the
   refusals are listed at the end of this comment.

   The format. A TZif file is a 44-octet header, a variable-length data block,
   and -- for version 2 and later -- a second header, a second data block with
   64-bit transition times, and a footer. The version octet says which: 0x00 is a
   version 1 file, which ends after the first block; '2', '3' and '4' are later
   versions, whose first block exists only for obsolescent readers. Reading the
   first block to get at the second would be reading the same times at 32 bits,
   so this reader calculates the length of the first block from its header and
   skips it (RFC 8536 section 3.2 and Appendix A say to), then parses the second
   with 8-octet times. A version 1 file is parsed from its own block, and has no
   footer: its transitions are limited to 1901..2038 by the width of a 32-bit
   time, which is the reason the later versions exist.

   A data block is four parallel series: the transition times (ascending UT
   seconds), one type index per transition, the local time type records (a
   4-octet utoff, a one-octet isdst, a one-octet index into the designations),
   and the NUL-terminated designations. Leap-second records and the standard/wall
   and UT/local indicators follow, and this reader steps over both: the leap
   records are refused by name (below), and the indicators describe only how the
   transition times were *written*, which is not a question a reader has to
   answer. For an instant, the applicable type is the one the last transition at
   or before it opened; before the first transition it is the first type that is
   not DST (which in every file of the database this was written against is type
   0, so the two readings of RFC 8536 and tzfile(5) agree here); and, when there
   is a footer, at or after the last transition it is the footer that decides.

   The footer is why this file exists in the shape it does. For most zones the
   last transition in the file is in the past -- Asia/Taipei's is 1979, Europe/
   Moscow's is 2014, New York's is 2037 -- so the offset for *now*, and every
   offset after it, comes from the POSIX TZ string in the footer rather than from
   the table. A reader that stops at the table is wrong about the present, and
   wrong in the direction that looks right: it answers the last rule the zone
   ever changed under, for ever. The string is whatever zic wrote, and this
   reader parses the whole of POSIX's grammar for it plus RFC 8536's version 3
   extensions:

     * the two names, quoted <like this> (the form tzdata uses for the zones
       whose abbreviation is not a name at all: <+0545>, <-02>) or unquoted and
       at least three letters (CST, EST, ChST);
     * the two offsets, in the printed form hh[:mm[:ss]] and in POSIX's inverted
       sense: CST-8 is +08:00 east of UT and EST5 is -05:00. The seconds default
       to zero, the DST offset defaults to one hour more than the standard one,
       and an offset of more than 24 hours is refused;
     * the two transition rules, in all three of POSIX's forms: Jn (day n of the
       year, January 1 is 1, February 29 is never counted), n (day n of the year
       counted from zero, February 29 counted), and Mm.w.d (month m, week w of
       that month -- 5 is the last one -- on weekday d, Sunday is 0);
     * the optional /time after either rule, defaulting to 02:00:00, given in the
       wall time in force just before the transition (standard time for the start
       of DST, DST for the end of it), which is what makes America/Nuuk's
       M3.5.0/-1 mean 23:00 the day before;
     * the version 3 extension that lets that time be negative or more than a
       day: the hours may be -167..167, which is how a zone whose clocks move at
       midnight or before it is written (America/Santiago's M9.1.6/24,
       Africa/Cairo's M4.5.4/24, America/Nuuk's M3.5.0/-1).

   Where the offsets, the DST flags and the names come from, in one sentence:
   from the type table for every instant the table covers, from the footer rule
   for every instant at or after the last transition, and for the instants before
   the first transition from the first type. The abbreviation is the designation
   the file names for that type, or the name on the appropriate side of the
   footer rule -- the string the zone's own data carries, which is what the C
   library's tm_zone answers and what a log line wants.

   What is deliberately not here, and why each is a decision rather than an
   omission.

   * The parts of java.time.zone that describe a rule *object* rather than
     answer a question about one: ZoneOffsetTransitionRule, and with it
     ZoneRules.getTransitionRules (which returns those rules). This reader keeps
     the parsed POSIX string instead -- PosixTz, which is not a public type of
     java.time's -- and it answers the same questions (which half of the rule an
     instant is in, and the two transitions of a year) without a second public
     shape to keep in step. ZoneRules.toString, which in Java prints a one-line
     summary of the current standard offset, is not here either; a program that
     wants that prints getStandardOffset.

   * ZoneId.getDisplayName and ZoneId.SHORT_IDS. The first needs the locale data
     of the zone names (CLDR), which this library does not have and which would
     answer a name the files do not carry; the second is a table of three-letter
     aliases that no part of this reader needs.

   * java.util.TimeZone is not implemented. It is the pre-java.time class for the
     same thing, and this library's whole date and time layer is java.time's:
     there is no Date and no Calendar for it to be handed to, its getOffset(int,
     int, int, int, int, int) is the Calendar-era signature that needs era rules
     this library has no other use for, and its surface is otherwise a subset of
     ZoneId's and ZoneRules'. The shapes that fit this library are the java.time
     ones, so ZoneId.getAvailableZoneIds() is the list of ids,
     ZoneId.systemDefault() is the default zone, and ZoneRules.getOffset is the
     offset for an instant -- the three things TimeZone would have been asked
     for. ZoneId.getAvailableIDs() answers the same list as a sorted String[],
     which is the shape java.util.TimeZone.getAvailableIDs has, because a sorted
     array is what a script wants and a stable thing for a test to print.
     ZoneId.setSystemDefault and ZoneId.setZoneInfoDir are this library's own
     additions, in the shape TimeZone.setDefault and ZoneRulesProvider have.

   * Leap seconds. A file whose selected data block carries leap-second records
     is refused by name: the transition times in such a file ("right/" in a
     tzdata installation) are UNIX *leap* seconds, so a reader that ignores the
     corrections would be off by the number of leap seconds after 1972 -- one
     answer that is wrong by 27 seconds is worse than no answer. No file under
     the ordinary tree has any, so nothing a program normally asks for is
     refused. The distance between TAI and UTC is not something this library
     claims to model.

   * A DST half with no transition rules, as in a bare "EST5EDT". POSIX leaves
     the rules to the implementation (glibc reads them from a file it keeps for
     that purpose), so there is nothing in the file to be right about; refused by
     name. No zone in a tzdata 2026 installation writes one.

   * A version octet other than 0x00, '2', '3' or '4'; a data block that runs
     past the end of the file; a type index outside the type table; a
     designation index outside the designations; a designation with no NUL;
     transition times that are not strictly ascending; an isdst octet that is
     neither 0 nor 1; a local time type offset outside the +/-18:00 a ZoneOffset
     here can carry. Each is refused with a message naming the file and the
     field. The point is the same in every case: a malformed file is a broken
     answer to a question about time, and the honest thing to do with one is to
     say so.

   * The values that a *later* tzdata would change. This reader answers from the
     host's files; the JVM answers from its own copy of the database, which can
     be a different vintage. Where they agree -- every case t188 pins, all of
     them fixed instants from the historical record -- they agree exactly; where
     the host's data is newer than the JVM's, the host's is the one that
     describes the machine the program is running on.

   Two places where this file deliberately answers differently from a JVM, both
   because the data is not the same data.

   * Negative DST. tzdata models Ireland's winter as DST with -1 hour of saving
     (the footer of Europe/Dublin is IST-1GMT0,M10.5.0,M3.5.0/1) and marks the
     winter type isdst=1. The JVM's copy of the database flattens that: it
     answers isDaylightSavings false for a January instant and calls the winter
     the standard offset. This library follows the file -- the flag is the octet
     the type carries and the standard offset is the offset of the file's most
     recent type that is not DST -- so on that file Dublin's winter is DST here,
     its standard offset is IST (+01:00) all year, and glibc's localtime agrees
     with this library rather than with the JVM. The offsets themselves are the
     same either way; only the flag and the standard offset differ.

     What decides is the file and not the zone, because tzdata is built in two
     layouts and they disagree about this one flag. Debian and Ubuntu build it
     from the *rearguard* layout, where Ireland is GMT standard with IST as the
     isdst type: there this library answers `std` for a January instant, because
     that is what those files say, and a program that compares its answer against
     a machine running the vanguard layout will see the two disagree. Both answers
     are the host's file read correctly, which is why the tests pin the layouts
     and not the zone: tests/programs/t190_timezone_tzif.teyru writes Ireland in
     both layouts as files it owns, and tests/programs/t189_timezone_lookup.teyru
     asserts the offsets and designations, which the two layouts agree on.

   * Abbreviations. The JVM has no API that answers a tzfile's designation:
     DateTimeFormatter's "z" and ZoneId.getDisplayName answer CLDR's name for the
     zone, which is "NPT" for Asia/Kathmandu where the file says "+0545", "WGST"
     for America/Nuuk where the file says "-01", and "CST" for the exact era in
     which the file says "JST". ZoneRules.getAbbreviation here answers the file.

   The one security-shaped thing in this file is the id. A zone id is a path
   under the search directory, and an id that begins with '/' or contains ".." is
   refused with ZoneRulesException before any file is opened: "Asia/Taipei" is a
   name, "../../etc/passwd" is not, and the difference has to be decided by a
   reader rather than by whatever open() does with it. Every other id is checked
   the way ZoneId.of checks it in Java (letters, digits, and '/', '.', '_', '-',
   '+', '~' after the first character), and an id the database does not have is
   the JVM's own message: "Unknown time-zone ID: <id>".

   Which tests pin which claim. tests/programs/t188_timezone.teyru is the same
   program as the javac 21 program in t188_timezone.java.ref, and its
   expectations are that program's output on a JVM: the offsets, the DST flags,
   the gap and overlap answers, the transition boundaries, the arithmetic across
   a boundary and the toString of a zoned date are all Java's answers, and the
   zones were chosen so that the host's files and the JVM's data agree on them.
   tests/programs/t189_timezone_lookup.teyru is the policy side: the id list, the
   refusals, TZDIR and TZ, and the abbreviations that are the *file's* answers
   rather than a JVM's. tests/programs/t190_timezone_tzif.teyru writes the files it
   reads, so that the format's own corners are held still -- a version 1 file, a
   version 2 file whose two blocks disagree, an empty footer, a footer with a DST
   half and no rules, a file with leap-second records, one with the indicator
   series -- and then checks the host's odd rules past the end of every table
   (Cairo's 00:00 and 24:00 transition times, Gaza's 50 hours, Chatham's 45
   minutes, Santiago's Saturday rule, Nuuk's negative time, Lord Howe's half hour
   of DST) against zdump, and the local-time answers for the gaps and overlaps
   those rules make against a JVM.

   Between them the three tests also carry the evidence this reader was written
   against. All 599 zones of a tzdata 2026b installation, at every transition of
   their history and every two years from 1890 to 2060 -- 175777 instants -- were
   read for their offset, DST flag and abbreviation and compared with glibc's
   answers for the same files: no difference. And 47169 local date-times in
   sixteen zones, chosen at the gaps and overlaps of every transition in them and
   in the footer era, were read for getValidOffsets, getTransition and
   ZonedDateTime.of and compared with a JVM's: no difference either.

   One thing this file does not fix, because it is not this file's to fix:
   lib/20_time.teyru says at the top that there is no zone database and that
   LocalDate.now() and LocalDateTime.now() read the clock as UTC. Those two still
   do -- this file adds the zone layer beside them rather than changing them --
   so LocalDateTime.now() is UTC while ZonedDateTime.now() is in the default
   zone. */

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the exception

/* java.time.zone.ZoneRulesException, which extends DateTimeException, which
   extends RuntimeException. The hierarchy is Java's, and this class is what
   every refusal below raises: a zone that is not there, a file that is not a
   zone file, a file that is a broken one, an id that is a path traversal. */
class ZoneRulesException extends DateTimeException {
  public ZoneRulesException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the arithmetic both halves need

/* TzMath is the one conversion this file needs everywhere: between a UT second
   and the local date and time an offset makes of it, and between a local date
   and time and the UT second it would be at zero offset. A transition in a TZif
   file is a UT second and the local time on either side of it is that second
   plus the offset on that side, so every question about a gap or an overlap is
   asked on the local second and comes back through here.

   It is a final class holding nothing but statics, as this library's other
   helpers are: a method needs a class to live in, and there is no state in
   these. */
final class TzMath {
  public static final long SECONDS_PER_DAY = 86400L

  // The local second of a date-time, counted from 1970-01-01T00:00 in the local
  // calendar with no offset applied: the same number as the epoch second of the
  // date-time read as if it were UT, which is the number a transition's local
  // boundary is compared against.
  public static long localSecond(LocalDateTime dt) {
    return dt.toLocalDate().toEpochDay() * SECONDS_PER_DAY + dt.toLocalTime().toSecondOfDay()
  }

  public static LocalDateTime localDateTime(long second, int nanos, int offsetSeconds) {
    long local = second + offsetSeconds
    long day = Math.floorDiv(local, SECONDS_PER_DAY)
    long s = Math.floorMod(local, SECONDS_PER_DAY)
    LocalDate date = LocalDate.ofEpochDay(day)
    LocalTime time = LocalTime.of((int) (s / 3600), (int) ((s % 3600) / 60), (int) (s % 60), nanos)
    return LocalDateTime.of(date, time)
  }

  public static LocalDateTime localDateTime(long second, int offsetSeconds) {
    return localDateTime(second, 0, offsetSeconds)
  }

  /* A local date-time moved by whole seconds while keeping its fraction. The gap
     of an hour that a spring-forward transition leaves is closed by moving a
     local time through it, and LocalDateTime.plusSeconds cannot do it: it goes
     through LocalTime.plusSeconds, which rebuilds the time from the second of
     the day and drops the nanos. A zoned time of 01:30:00.500 in a gap has to
     come out as 03:30:00.500. */
  public static LocalDateTime plusSeconds(LocalDateTime dt, long seconds) {
    long local = localSecond(dt) + seconds
    long day = Math.floorDiv(local, SECONDS_PER_DAY)
    long s = Math.floorMod(local, SECONDS_PER_DAY)
    int nano = dt.toLocalTime().getNano()
    LocalDate date = LocalDate.ofEpochDay(day)
    LocalTime time = LocalTime.of((int) (s / 3600), (int) ((s % 3600) / 60), (int) (s % 60), nano)
    return LocalDateTime.of(date, time)
  }

  /* The year a UT second falls in, read in the local calendar of the offset
     passed with it: a DST rule is a rule about a year, and which year is the
     question that has to be answered before either transition of it can be
     found. */
  public static int yearOf(long second, int offsetSeconds) {
    return (int) TimeMath.civilFromDays(Math.floorDiv(second + offsetSeconds, SECONDS_PER_DAY))[0]
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ZoneId, and ZoneOffset under it

/* java.time.ZoneId: a name for a set of rules. Two kinds of name live under it,
   as in Java. A region id names a file in the zone database, and its rules are
   read from that file the first time they are asked for. An offset id -- "Z",
   "+08:00", "UTC", "GMT", "UTC+05:45" -- names an offset with no database behind
   it, and ZoneOffset, its subclass here as it is in Java, is what those answer
   with.

   ZoneOffset extends ZoneId rather than standing beside it because that is what
   Java does and because it is what makes ZonedDateTime.of(local, ZoneOffset.UTC)
   compile: a ZoneOffset *is* a ZoneId, so one method takes both. The subclass
   keeps its own totalSeconds, inherits the id, and overrides getRules to build a
   fixed rule set -- exactly the split the JDK has.

   One shape this class has that Java's does not: it is Comparable, ordered by
   id, because an id is a string and a list of zones sorted by name is a thing
   programs want (Java's ZoneId has no order, which is why sorting zones there
   needs a Comparator). ZoneOffset's own compareTo, by total seconds, is Java's.

   The three static things that are not in a JVM. TZDIR is honoured, because that
   is where the C library reads its zone files from and this reader reads the C
   library's files: set it and the search path is that directory, unset it and it
   is /usr/share/zoneinfo. TZ is honoured for the default zone, which is POSIX's
   rule and not Java's -- a JVM ignores TZ entirely -- so a program run with
   TZ=Asia/Taipei behaves like a C program run with it, whether TZ names a zone
   ("Asia/Taipei", ":Asia/Taipei") or is the rule itself ("CST-8"). With TZ unset
   the default comes from /etc/localtime. And setZoneInfoDir is this library's
   own: a way to point the reader at another database, which is what TZDIR does
   for a C library and what java.time.zone.ZoneRulesProvider does, less
   elaborately, for a JVM. */
class ZoneId implements Comparable<ZoneId> {
  private static final String DEFAULT_ZONE_INFO_DIR = "/usr/share/zoneinfo"
  private static final String LOCAL_TIME_FILE = "/etc/localtime"
  // The errno of a file that is not there, which is the one answer of a read
  // that means "no such zone" rather than "this file cannot be read at all".
  private static final int ENOENT = 2

  /* Package-level, not private, and the same fields a subclass would inherit in
     Java: ZoneOffset sets the id through its super constructor and has no id of
     its own, and both classes cache the rules under this name. */
  String id
  ZoneRules rules

  // The search path when the environment has not named one, the cache of files
  // already read, the id list, the environment's default zone once it has been
  // worked out, and the process's override of it.
  private static String zoneInfoDir = null
  private static Map<String, ZoneRules> cache = null
  private static String[] available = null
  private static Set<String> availableSet = null
  private static ZoneId environmentDefault = null
  private static ZoneId overriddenDefault = null

  ZoneId(String id) {
    this.id = id
  }

  ZoneId(String id, ZoneRules rules) {
    this.id = id
    this.rules = rules
  }

  // ---- the shape of an id ----

  /* Java's ZoneRegion.checkName, message for message. It is what makes
     ZoneId.of("Asia Taipei") a DateTimeException rather than a failed lookup,
     and what the traversal rule below is *not*: this one admits ".." and a
     leading '/', so the check that refuses those is separate and runs first. */
  private static void checkName(String id) {
    int n = id.length()
    if (n < 2) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for region-based ZoneId, invalid format: " + id)
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < n : i++) {
      char c = id.charAt(i)
      if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
        continue
      }
      if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '/' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c >= '0' && c <= '9' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '~' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '.' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '_' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '+' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (c == '-' && i != 0) {
        continue
      }
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for region-based ZoneId, invalid format: " + id)
    }
  }

  /* The one rule of this file that is about a path rather than about time. A
     zone id is turned into a path under the search directory, so an id that
     starts with '/' would leave that directory and an id holding ".." could walk
     out of it; both are refused before anything is opened, with a message that
     says which of the two it was. Java does not have this check -- it admits
     ".." and fails the lookup afterwards, and it rejects a leading '/' only as a
     malformed id -- so this is this library's own and its tests state it as
     such. */
  private static void checkSafe(String id) {
    if (id.startsWith("/")) {
      String why = " (a zone id is a path under the zone directory, so it cannot be absolute)"
      throw new ZoneRulesException("Invalid zone id: " + id + why)
    }
    if (id.contains("..")) {
      String why = " (a zone id is a path under the zone directory, so it cannot contain \"..\")"
      throw new ZoneRulesException("Invalid zone id: " + id + why)
    }
  }

  // ---- the search path ----

  /* Where the zone files are. TZDIR is the C library's variable and means the
     same thing here: the directory the zone files are looked up in, when it is
     set and not empty. */
  public static String getZoneInfoDir() {
    if (zoneInfoDir != null) {
      return zoneInfoDir
    }
    String env = System.getenv("TZDIR")
    if (env != null && env.length() > 0) {
      return env
    }
    return DEFAULT_ZONE_INFO_DIR
  }

  /* Point the reader at another database, or pass null to go back to what the
     environment says. This is this library's own addition -- glibc has no such
     call and the JDK's equivalent is ZoneRulesProvider.registerProvider -- and
     it is what lets a test use a directory of its own. */
  public static void setZoneInfoDir(String dir) {
    zoneInfoDir = dir
    cache = null
    available = null
    availableSet = null
    environmentDefault = null
  }

  private static Map<String, ZoneRules> rulesCache() {
    if (cache == null) {
      cache = new HashMap<String, ZoneRules>()
    }
    return cache
  }

  /* The message every operation that needs the database gives when there is not
     one. There is no fallback data, so this is where a Windows host or a
     container without tzdata ends up, and the message says what to install or
     set rather than failing later with a wrong offset. */
  private static ZoneRulesException noDatabase(String dir) {
    StringBuilder why = new StringBuilder()
    why.append("no time zone database: ").append(dir).append(" is not a directory")
    why.append(" (this library reads the system's own TZif files")
    why.append("; install tzdata, or set TZDIR to where they are)")
    return new ZoneRulesException(why.toString())
  }

  private static ZoneRules load(String id) {
    String dir = getZoneInfoDir()
    if (Fs.kind0(dir) != Fs.TY_FILE_DIR) {
      throw noDatabase(dir)
    }
    String path = dir + "/" + id
    /* The kind first, because a directory named like a zone is not a zone and a
       file that cannot be read is not an unknown zone: the first is the JVM's
       "Unknown time-zone ID", the second a failure with the errno in it. */
    int kind = Fs.kind0(path)
    if (kind == Fs.TY_FILE_NONE || (kind > 0 && kind != Fs.TY_FILE_REG)) {
      throw new ZoneRulesException("Unknown time-zone ID: " + id)
    }
    if (kind < 0) {
      throw new ZoneRulesException("cannot read " + path + ": " + Net.strerror(kind))
    }
    int[] err = new int[1]
    byte[] data = Fs.readBytes0(path, err)
    if (data == null) {
      if (err[0] == ENOENT) {
        throw new ZoneRulesException("Unknown time-zone ID: " + id)
      }
      throw new ZoneRulesException("cannot read " + path + ": " + Net.strerror(err[0]))
    }
    return new ZoneRules(TzifData.parse(data, path))
  }

  private static ZoneRules rulesFor(String id) {
    Map<String, ZoneRules> c = rulesCache()
    ZoneRules r = c.get(id)
    if (r == null) {
      r = load(id)
      c.put(id, r)
    }
    return r
  }

  // ---- the list of ids ----

  private static boolean isTzif(String path) {
    try {
      byte[] b = Files.readAllBytes(path)
      return b.length >= 4 && b[0] == 'T' && b[1] == 'Z' && b[2] == 'i' && b[3] == 'f'
    } catch (IOException e) {
      // A file that cannot be read is not a zone this list can offer. Looking
      // that id up will say why in its own words.
      return false
    }
  }

  /* The ids are the files under the search path whose first four octets are the
     TZif magic. That is what makes the list the *system's* rather than a
     compiled-in one: a distribution that ships a zone this library has never
     heard of gets it for free, and the files that are not zones -- tzdata.zi,
     zone.tab, zone1970.tab, iso3166.tab, leapseconds, leap-seconds.list -- are
     exactly the ones the magic test leaves out, so no list of names has to be
     kept correct over time.

     The two alternate trees are skipped. posix/ and right/ hold the same zones
     in other representations (right/ carries the leap-second corrections), and
     neither is an id in glibc's sense: TZ=CET does not find posix/CET. */
  private static void collect(String path, String prefix, List<String> out) {
    String[] names = new File(path).list()
    if (names == null) {
      return
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < names.length : i++) {
      String name = names[i]
      String child = path + "/" + name
      String id = prefix.length() == 0 ? name : prefix + "/" + name
      int kind = Fs.kind0(child)
      if (kind == Fs.TY_FILE_DIR) {
        if (prefix.length() == 0 && (name.equals("posix") || name.equals("right"))) {
          continue
        }
        collect(child, id, out)
      } else if (kind == Fs.TY_FILE_REG && isTzif(child)) {
        out.add(id)
      }
    }
  }

  private static String[] idList() {
    if (available == null) {
      String dir = getZoneInfoDir()
      if (Fs.kind0(dir) != Fs.TY_FILE_DIR) {
        throw noDatabase(dir)
      }
      List<String> out = new ArrayList<String>()
      collect(dir, "", out)
      String[] ids = new String[out.size()]
      for (int i = 0 : i < out.size() : i++) {
        ids[i] = out.get(i)
      }
      Arrays.sort(ids)
      available = ids
    }
    return available
  }

  /* The Set java.time answers with, as a TreeSet so that it has an order: Java's
     own Set is unordered, which is why a program that prints it gets whatever
     its hash order happens to be. */
  public static Set<String> getAvailableZoneIds() {
    if (availableSet == null) {
      String[] ids = idList()
      Set<String> out = new TreeSet<String>()
      for (int i = 0 : i < ids.length : i++) {
        out.add(ids[i])
      }
      availableSet = out
    }
    return availableSet
  }

  /* The same list as a sorted array. java.util.TimeZone.getAvailableIDs is where
     this shape comes from; java.time has only the Set. */
  public static String[] getAvailableIDs() {
    return idList()
  }

  // ---- the names ----

  /* The prefix forms: "UTC", "GMT", "UT", and the same three with an offset
     after them ("UTC+08:00"). All three are built in rather than looked up, so a
     program that only needs UTC works on a host with no tzdata -- which is the
     case ZoneOffset.UTC also covers. An offset of zero after a prefix folds back
     to the bare prefix ("UTC+00:00" is "UTC"), as in Java. */
  private static ZoneId withPrefix(String id, int length) {
    String prefix = id.substring(0, length)
    if (id.length() == length) {
      return new ZoneId(prefix, ZoneRules.ofNamedOffset(ZoneOffset.UTC, prefix))
    }
    char next = id.charAt(length)
    if (next != '+' && next != '-') {
      return region(id)
    }
    ZoneOffset offset
    try {
      offset = ZoneOffset.of(id.substring(length))
    } catch (DateTimeException e) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for offset-based ZoneId: " + id)
    }
    if (offset.getTotalSeconds() == 0) {
      return new ZoneId(prefix, ZoneRules.ofNamedOffset(ZoneOffset.UTC, prefix))
    }
    return new ZoneId(prefix + offset.getId(), ZoneRules.ofNamedOffset(offset, prefix + offset.getId()))
  }

  private static ZoneId region(String id) {
    checkSafe(id)
    checkName(id)
    return new ZoneId(id, rulesFor(id))
  }

  /* Java's ZoneId.of, branch for branch: an id of one character or one that
     starts with a sign is an offset; UTC, GMT and UT are the three prefixes; and
     everything else is a region. The id a caller writes comes back from getId
     unchanged, so ZoneId.of("Europe/Kiev") is still named Europe/Kiev even
     though the file it reads is the one Europe/Kyiv links to. */
  public static ZoneId of(String id) {
    if (id == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("zoneId")
    }
    if (id.length() <= 1 || id.startsWith("+") || id.startsWith("-")) {
      return ZoneOffset.of(id)
    }
    if (id.startsWith("UTC") || id.startsWith("GMT")) {
      return withPrefix(id, 3)
    }
    if (id.startsWith("UT")) {
      return withPrefix(id, 2)
    }
    return region(id)
  }

  public String getId() {
    return id
  }

  public String toString() {
    return id
  }

  public ZoneRules getRules() {
    if (rules == null) {
      rules = ZoneId.rulesFor(id)
    }
    return rules
  }

  /* Java's: the ZoneOffset of a zone whose rules never move, and this, itself,
     for one that does. ZoneId.of("UTC").normalized() is ZoneOffset.UTC, which
     prints as Z. */
  public ZoneId normalized() {
    ZoneRules r = getRules()
    if (r.isFixedOffset()) {
      return r.getOffset(Instant.ofEpochSecond(0))
    }
    return this
  }

  /* Two ids are equal when they are the same characters, and an offset is never
     equal to a region with the same name: ZoneId.of("UTC").equals(ZoneOffset.UTC)
     is false, as in Java, because they are different kinds of name for the same
     instant. */
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
      return true
    }
    if (o instanceof ZoneOffset) {
      return false
    }
    if (o instanceof ZoneId) {
      return id.equals(((ZoneId) o).id)
    }
    return false
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return id.hashCode()
  }

  public int compareTo(ZoneId other) {
    return id.compareTo(other.id)
  }

  // ---- the default zone ----

  /* The id of the zone whose file is byte for byte the file at /etc/localtime,
     or null when none is. A JVM learns the default zone's *name* from native
     code that follows the /etc/localtime link; there is no readlink in this
     library, so the name is found by content instead: the ids are searched in
     sorted order and the first file that matches is the answer. Two ids can name
     the same file (Europe/Kiev is a link to Europe/Kyiv in the database this was
     written against), and then the first in sorted order wins, which is a stable
     answer for a name the file itself does not carry. */
  private static String matchingId(byte[] data) {
    String dir = getZoneInfoDir()
    String[] ids = idList()
    for (int i = 0 : i < ids.length : i++) {
      String path = dir + "/" + ids[i]
      if (Fs.size0(path) != data.length) {
        continue
      }
      try {
        byte[] other = Files.readAllBytes(path)
        if (other.length == data.length) {
          boolean same = true
          for (int k = 0 : k < data.length : k++) {
            if (other[k] != data[k]) {
              same = false
              break
            }
          }
          if (same) {
            return ids[i]
          }
        }
      } catch (IOException e) {
        // A file this reader cannot compare cannot be the one asked for.
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  private static ZoneId fromLocalTimeFile() {
    String dir = getZoneInfoDir()
    if (Fs.kind0(dir) != Fs.TY_FILE_DIR) {
      throw noDatabase(dir)
    }
    int kind = Fs.kind0(LOCAL_TIME_FILE)
    if (kind == Fs.TY_FILE_NONE) {
      // No /etc/localtime at all: UTC, which is what the C library falls back to
      // as well.
      return of("UTC")
    }
    if (kind < 0) {
      throw new ZoneRulesException("cannot read " + LOCAL_TIME_FILE + ": " + Net.strerror(kind))
    }
    int[] err = new int[1]
    byte[] data = Fs.readBytes0(LOCAL_TIME_FILE, err)
    if (data == null) {
      throw new ZoneRulesException("cannot read " + LOCAL_TIME_FILE + ": " + Net.strerror(err[0]))
    }
    String id = matchingId(data)
    if (id == null) {
      StringBuilder why = new StringBuilder()
      why.append(LOCAL_TIME_FILE).append(" is not the same file as any zone under ").append(dir)
      why.append(" (there is no readlink here, so the local zone is named by its contents:")
      why.append(" set TZ to the id of the zone /etc/localtime is a copy of)")
      throw new ZoneRulesException(why.toString())
    }
    return of(id)
  }

  /* TZ, then /etc/localtime, then UTC -- POSIX's order and POSIX's meanings,
     which are not Java's: a JVM ignores TZ and reads its own database. An empty
     TZ is UTC, TZ=":name" is the same as TZ="name", and a TZ that names no zone
     in the database is read as the rule itself, which is glibc's other form. */
  private static ZoneId resolveDefault() {
    String tz = System.getenv("TZ")
    if (tz != null && tz.length() == 0) {
      return of("UTC")
    }
    if (tz != null) {
      String name = tz
      if (name.startsWith(":")) {
        name = name.substring(1)
      }
      try {
        return of(name)
      } catch (DateTimeException e) {
        PosixTz parsed = PosixTz.parse(tz, "TZ=" + tz)
        return new ZoneId(tz, ZoneRules.fromPosix(parsed))
      }
    }
    return fromLocalTimeFile()
  }

  public static ZoneId systemDefault() {
    if (overriddenDefault != null) {
      return overriddenDefault
    }
    if (environmentDefault == null) {
      environmentDefault = resolveDefault()
    }
    return environmentDefault
  }

  /* This library's addition, the shape java.util.TimeZone.setDefault has: hand
     the process a default zone, or hand it null to go back to the environment's
     answer. */
  public static void setSystemDefault(ZoneId zone) {
    overriddenDefault = zone
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ZoneOffset

/* java.time.ZoneOffset: a fixed offset from UT, in whole seconds, and the only
   zone whose offset does not depend on when it is asked.

   The range is Java's +/-(18:00), which is wider than any civil offset and
   narrower than what a TZif file may carry (RFC 8536 allows -25 to +26 hours,
   and this reader refuses a file that uses one: an offset this class cannot hold
   would otherwise have to be answered with a number it would have to check
   anyway). The printed form is Java's too: "Z" for zero, "+HH:MM" to the minute,
   and "+HH:MM:SS" when there are seconds to print, with a negative sub-minute
   offset printing as "-00:00:30" rather than losing its sign. */
class ZoneOffset extends ZoneId {
  private static final int MIN_TOTAL_SECONDS = -18 * 3600
  private static final int MAX_TOTAL_SECONDS = 18 * 3600

  public static final ZoneOffset UTC = new ZoneOffset(0)
  public static final ZoneOffset MIN = new ZoneOffset(MIN_TOTAL_SECONDS)
  public static final ZoneOffset MAX = new ZoneOffset(MAX_TOTAL_SECONDS)

  private final int totalSeconds

  private ZoneOffset(int totalSeconds) {
    super(ZoneOffset.buildId(totalSeconds))
    this.totalSeconds = totalSeconds
  }

  private static String buildId(int totalSeconds) {
    if (totalSeconds == 0) {
      return "Z"
    }
    int abs = totalSeconds < 0 ? -totalSeconds : totalSeconds
    StringBuilder b = new StringBuilder()
    b.append(totalSeconds < 0 ? "-" : "+")
    int hours = abs / 3600
    int minutes = (abs / 60) % 60
    if (hours < 10) {
      b.append("0")
    }
    b.append(hours)
    if (minutes < 10) {
      b.append(":0")
    } else {
      b.append(":")
    }
    b.append(minutes)
    int seconds = abs % 60
    if (seconds != 0) {
      if (seconds < 10) {
        b.append(":0")
      } else {
        b.append(":")
      }
      b.append(seconds)
    }
    return b.toString()
  }

  // Java's parseNumber: two digits at a position. The messages are Java's, so a
  // caller that prints what a bad id raised gets the same sentence a JVM does.
  private static int parseNumber(String id, int pos) {
    char c1 = id.charAt(pos)
    char c2 = id.charAt(pos + 1)
    if (c1 < '0' || c1 > '9' || c2 < '0' || c2 > '9') {
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for ZoneOffset, non numeric characters found: " + id)
    }
    return (c1 - 48) * 10 + (c2 - 48)
  }

  private static void validate(int hours, int minutes, int seconds) {
    if (hours < -18 || hours > 18) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset hours not in valid range: value " + hours + " is not in the range -18 to 18")
    }
    if (minutes < -59 || minutes > 59) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset minutes not in valid range: value " + minutes + " is not in the range -59 to 59")
    }
    if (seconds < -59 || seconds > 59) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset seconds not in valid range: value " + seconds + " is not in the range -59 to 59")
    }
    if (hours > 0 && (minutes < 0 || seconds < 0)) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset minutes and seconds must be positive because hours is positive")
    }
    if (hours < 0 && (minutes > 0 || seconds > 0)) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset minutes and seconds must be negative because hours is negative")
    }
    if (hours == 0 && ((minutes > 0 && seconds < 0) || (minutes < 0 && seconds > 0))) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset minutes and seconds must have the same sign")
    }
  }

  public static ZoneOffset ofTotalSeconds(int totalSeconds) {
    if (totalSeconds < MIN_TOTAL_SECONDS || totalSeconds > MAX_TOTAL_SECONDS) {
      throw new DateTimeException("Zone offset not in valid range: -18:00 to +18:00")
    }
    if (totalSeconds == 0) {
      return UTC
    }
    return new ZoneOffset(totalSeconds)
  }

  public static ZoneOffset ofHoursMinutesSeconds(int hours, int minutes, int seconds) {
    validate(hours, minutes, seconds)
    return ofTotalSeconds(hours * 3600 + minutes * 60 + seconds)
  }

  public static ZoneOffset ofHoursMinutes(int hours, int minutes) {
    return ofHoursMinutesSeconds(hours, minutes, 0)
  }

  public static ZoneOffset ofHours(int hours) {
    return ofHoursMinutesSeconds(hours, 0, 0)
  }

  /* The id forms Java accepts, and only those: Z; +h and +hh (three characters
     after the sign is folded in); +hhmm (five); +hh:mm (six); +hhmmss (seven);
     and +hh:mm:ss (nine). Eight characters is not a form, which is what makes
     "+0800:00" and "+08:0000" invalid rather than half-read. Anything else is a
     DateTimeException whose message is Java's. */
  public static ZoneOffset of(String offsetId) {
    if (offsetId == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("offsetId")
    }
    if (offsetId.equals("Z")) {
      return UTC
    }
    String text = offsetId
    int hours
    int minutes = 0
    int seconds = 0
    int length = text.length()
    if (length == 2) {
      text = text.substring(0, 1) + "0" + text.substring(1)
      length = 3
    }
    if (length == 3) {
      hours = parseNumber(text, 1)
    } else if (length == 5) {
      hours = parseNumber(text, 1)
      minutes = parseNumber(text, 3)
    } else if (length == 6) {
      hours = parseNumber(text, 1)
      minutes = parseNumber(text, 4)
    } else if (length == 7) {
      hours = parseNumber(text, 1)
      minutes = parseNumber(text, 3)
      seconds = parseNumber(text, 5)
    } else if (length == 9) {
      hours = parseNumber(text, 1)
      minutes = parseNumber(text, 4)
      seconds = parseNumber(text, 7)
    } else {
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for ZoneOffset, invalid format: " + offsetId)
    }
    char first = text.charAt(0)
    if (first != '+' && first != '-') {
      throw new DateTimeException("Invalid ID for ZoneOffset, plus/minus not found when expected: " + offsetId)
    }
    if (first == '-') {
      return ofHoursMinutesSeconds(-hours, -minutes, -seconds)
    }
    return ofHoursMinutesSeconds(hours, minutes, seconds)
  }

  public int getTotalSeconds() {
    return totalSeconds
  }

  public String getId() {
    return id
  }

  public String toString() {
    return id
  }

  /* A fixed rule set whose one offset is *this* object, which is what makes the
     identity test in ZonedDateTime.toString work: an offset zone's zone and its
     offset are the same object, so no "[+08:00]" is printed after it. */
  public ZoneRules getRules() {
    if (rules == null) {
      rules = ZoneRules.fixed(this)
    }
    return rules
  }

  public int compareTo(ZoneOffset other) {
    return totalSeconds - other.totalSeconds
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
      return true
    }
    if (o instanceof ZoneOffset) {
      return totalSeconds == ((ZoneOffset) o).totalSeconds
    }
    return false
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return totalSeconds
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the transition object

/* java.time.zone.ZoneOffsetTransition: one instant at which the offset changes,
   with the offset on either side of it. It is what a gap and an overlap *are* --
   the local times that do not exist are the ones between the two local times
   this object answers, and the local times that exist twice are the ones between
   them when the offset went backwards.

   The local date-time on either side is the instant plus that side's offset, and
   the toString is Java's own: "Transition[Gap at 2021-03-14T02:00-05:00 to
   -04:00]" -- the gap case of New York's spring forward, which is the string
   t188 pins. */
class ZoneOffsetTransition implements Comparable<ZoneOffsetTransition> {
  private final long epochSecond
  private final ZoneOffset before
  private final ZoneOffset after

  ZoneOffsetTransition(long epochSecond, ZoneOffset before, ZoneOffset after) {
    this.epochSecond = epochSecond
    this.before = before
    this.after = after
  }

  /* Java's factory, for a transition written as the local time it happened at
     plus the offset in force before it. */
  public static ZoneOffsetTransition of(LocalDateTime transition, ZoneOffset offsetBefore, ZoneOffset offsetAfter) {
    return new ZoneOffsetTransition(TzMath.localSecond(transition) - offsetBefore.getTotalSeconds(), offsetBefore, offsetAfter)
  }

  public long toEpochSecond() {
    return epochSecond
  }

  public Instant getInstant() {
    return Instant.ofEpochSecond(epochSecond)
  }

  public ZoneOffset getOffsetBefore() {
    return before
  }

  public ZoneOffset getOffsetAfter() {
    return after
  }

  public LocalDateTime getDateTimeBefore() {
    return TzMath.localDateTime(epochSecond, before.getTotalSeconds())
  }

  public LocalDateTime getDateTimeAfter() {
    return TzMath.localDateTime(epochSecond, after.getTotalSeconds())
  }

  public Duration getDuration() {
    return Duration.ofSeconds((long) after.getTotalSeconds() - before.getTotalSeconds())
  }

  // A gap is a transition the clocks jumped over, so the local time after it is
  // later than the local time before; an overlap is the reverse.
  public boolean isGap() {
    return after.getTotalSeconds() > before.getTotalSeconds()
  }

  public boolean isOverlap() {
    return after.getTotalSeconds() < before.getTotalSeconds()
  }

  /* Whether a local time with this offset is one of the two this transition
     makes ambiguous. A gap has no valid offset at all, which is why this is
     false for every offset when it is one. */
  public boolean isValidOffset(ZoneOffset offset) {
    if (isGap()) {
      return false
    }
    return before.equals(offset) || after.equals(offset)
  }

  public int compareTo(ZoneOffsetTransition other) {
    if (epochSecond < other.epochSecond) {
      return -1
    }
    return epochSecond > other.epochSecond ? 1 : 0
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
      return true
    }
    if (o instanceof ZoneOffsetTransition) {
      ZoneOffsetTransition d = (ZoneOffsetTransition) o
      return epochSecond == d.epochSecond && before.equals(d.before) && after.equals(d.after)
    }
    return false
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return (int) (epochSecond ^ before.hashCode() ^ Integer.rotateLeft(after.hashCode(), 16))
  }

  public String toString() {
    return "Transition[" + (isGap() ? "Gap" : "Overlap") + " at " + getDateTimeBefore() + before + " to " + after + "]"
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the footer rule

/* The POSIX TZ string that decides every instant at or after a file's last
   transition, and the same grammar for a TZ environment variable that names a
   rule rather than a zone.

   The grammar is the one in POSIX's TZ definition plus the extensions RFC 8536
   section 3.3.1 allows in a version 3 file, and all of it is parsed rather than
   the parts the current database happens to use, because the parts it does not
   use are the parts the next release may.

     name:      <quoted> or at least three letters. The quoted form is what a
                name with digits or a sign in it has to use -- <+0545>, <-02>,
                <+1030> -- and its contents may not hold a digit or a sign,
                which is POSIX's rule for it.
     offset:    [+-]h[h][:mm[:ss]], in POSIX's inverted sense: it counts west, so
                CST-8 is +08:00 east of UT. More than 24 hours is refused.
     dst:       the same, optional, and defaulting to one hour more than the
                standard offset.
     rule:      Jn (January 1 is 1, February 29 never counted), n (days counted
                from zero, February 29 counted), or Mm.w.d (month, week with 5
                meaning the last, weekday with Sunday 0).
     /time:     the wall time of the transition, in the offset in force just
                before it, defaulting to 02:00:00, and running from -167 to +167
                hours in a version 3 file: America/Nuuk's M3.5.0/-1 is 23:00 the
                day before and America/Santiago's M9.1.6/24 is midnight at the
                end of the day.

   What is *not* accepted, and refused by name: a DST half with no rule at all (a
   bare "EST5EDT"), where POSIX leaves the rule to the implementation and glibc
   reads a file this library does not have; an offset of more than 24 hours; a
   transition time outside -167..167 hours; a name shorter than three letters or
   a quoted one holding a digit; a month outside 1..12, a week outside 1..5, a
   weekday outside 0..6, a day of the year outside its range; anything left over
   at the end of the string. Each is a file that cannot be answered from, and the
   message names the part of the string that was wrong.

   Two rules of POSIX's that are not obvious, and both are implemented. The
   transition time is in the wall time of the *before* side (standard time for
   the start of DST, DST for the end), which is why a rule's two transitions are
   computed with different offsets. And the year a rule applies to is the year
   the *local* time falls in, which is why a southern-hemisphere rule (Sydney's
   AEST-10AEDT,M10.1.0,M4.1.0/3) is not simply one interval per year: this
   implementation asks the year and its two neighbours for their transitions and
   answers with the half the latest transition at or before the instant
   established, which is the same answer without the special cases. */
final class PosixTz {
  private static final int JULIAN_ONE = 0
  private static final int DAY_OF_YEAR = 1
  private static final int MONTH_WEEK_DAY = 2
  private static final int DEFAULT_DST_START = 7200

  private final String text
  private final String stdName
  private final int stdOffset
  private final String dstName
  private final int dstOffset
  private final int[] start
  private final int[] end

  private PosixTz(String text, String stdName, int stdOffset, String dstName, int dstOffset, int[] start, int[] end) {
    this.text = text
    this.stdName = stdName
    this.stdOffset = stdOffset
    this.dstName = dstName
    this.dstOffset = dstOffset
    this.start = start
    this.end = end
  }

  // ---- the parser ----

  private static ZoneRulesException bad(String where, String why) {
    return new ZoneRulesException("cannot read the TZ string in " + where + ": " + why)
  }

  private static int digits(String s, int[] pos, int max, String where) {
    int out = 0
    int n = 0
    while (n < max && pos[0] < s.length()) {
      char c = s.charAt(pos[0])
      if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
        break
      }
      out = out * 10 + (c - 48)
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      n = n + 1
    }
    if (n == 0) {
      throw bad(where, "a number is missing")
    }
    return out
  }

  private static String name(String s, int[] pos, String where) {
    if (pos[0] >= s.length()) {
      throw bad(where, "a name is missing")
    }
    char c = s.charAt(pos[0])
    if (c == '<') {
      int close = s.indexOf(62, pos[0] + 1)
      if (close < 0) {
        throw bad(where, "a quoted name is not closed")
      }
      String out = s.substring(pos[0] + 1, close)
      if (out.length() == 0) {
        throw bad(where, "a quoted name is empty")
      }
      pos[0] = close + 1
      return out
    }
    int from = pos[0]
    while (pos[0] < s.length()) {
      char q = s.charAt(pos[0])
      if ((q >= 'a' && q <= 'z') || (q >= 'A' && q <= 'Z')) {
        pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      } else {
        break
      }
    }
    if (pos[0] - from < 3) {
      throw bad(where, "a name is shorter than three letters")
    }
    return s.substring(from, pos[0])
  }

  /* hh[:mm[:ss]] with an optional sign, answered as the number the string
     carries: an *offset* in a TZ string is POSIX's, which counts west positive,
     so its two callers negate this and the transition time after '/' does not
     (that one is a wall clock, not a sense). */
  private static int clock(String s, int[] pos, String where, int maxHours, int maxDigits) {
    boolean west = false
    if (pos[0] < s.length()) {
      char c = s.charAt(pos[0])
      if (c == '+' || c == '-') {
        west = c == '-'
        pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      }
    }
    int hours = digits(s, pos, maxDigits, where)
    int minutes = 0
    int seconds = 0
    if (pos[0] < s.length() && s.charAt(pos[0]) == ':') {
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      minutes = digits(s, pos, 2, where)
      if (pos[0] < s.length() && s.charAt(pos[0]) == ':') {
        pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
        seconds = digits(s, pos, 2, where)
      }
    }
    if (hours > maxHours) {
      throw bad(where, "an offset of more than " + maxHours + " hours")
    }
    if (minutes > 59 || seconds > 59) {
      throw bad(where, "an offset with more than 59 minutes or seconds")
    }
    int total = hours * 3600 + minutes * 60 + seconds
    return west ? -total : total
  }

  // Jn, n or Mm.w.d, with the optional /time, as six numbers: the kind, the
  // month, the week, the weekday, the day of the year, and the time in seconds.
  private static int[] rule(String s, int[] pos, String where) {
    if (pos[0] >= s.length()) {
      throw bad(where, "a transition rule is missing")
    }
    char c = s.charAt(pos[0])
    int kind
    int month = 0
    int week = 0
    int day = 0
    int dayOfYear = 0
    if (c == 'J') {
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      kind = JULIAN_ONE
      dayOfYear = digits(s, pos, 3, where)
      if (dayOfYear < 1 || dayOfYear > 365) {
        throw bad(where, "a Julian day outside 1..365")
      }
    } else if (c == 'M') {
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      kind = MONTH_WEEK_DAY
      month = digits(s, pos, 2, where)
      if (pos[0] >= s.length() || s.charAt(pos[0]) != '.') {
        throw bad(where, "a month is not followed by '.'")
      }
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      week = digits(s, pos, 1, where)
      if (pos[0] >= s.length() || s.charAt(pos[0]) != '.') {
        throw bad(where, "a week is not followed by '.'")
      }
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      day = digits(s, pos, 1, where)
      if (month < 1 || month > 12) {
        throw bad(where, "a month outside 1..12")
      }
      if (week < 1 || week > 5) {
        throw bad(where, "a week outside 1..5")
      }
      if (day < 0 || day > 6) {
        throw bad(where, "a weekday outside 0..6")
      }
    } else {
      kind = DAY_OF_YEAR
      dayOfYear = digits(s, pos, 3, where)
      if (dayOfYear > 365) {
        throw bad(where, "a day of the year outside 0..365")
      }
    }
    int seconds = DEFAULT_DST_START
    if (pos[0] < s.length() && s.charAt(pos[0]) == '/') {
      pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
      seconds = clock(s, pos, where, 167, 3)
    }
    int[] out = new int[6]
    out[0] = kind
    out[1] = month
    out[2] = week
    out[3] = day
    out[4] = dayOfYear
    out[5] = seconds
    return out
  }

  /* Parse a whole TZ string. `where` names the thing the string came from -- a
     file path, or "TZ=..." for an environment variable -- because a message
     about a rule that cannot be read is useless without it. */
  public static PosixTz parse(String s, String where) {
    if (s.length() == 0) {
      throw bad(where, "the string is empty")
    }
    int[] pos = new int[1]
    String stdName = name(s, pos, where)
    // POSIX prints the offset west positive: CST-8 is eight hours east of UT.
    int stdOffset = -clock(s, pos, where, 24, 3)
    if (pos[0] >= s.length()) {
      return new PosixTz(s, stdName, stdOffset, null, 0, null, null)
    }
    String dstName = name(s, pos, where)
    int dstOffset = stdOffset + 3600
    if (pos[0] < s.length() && s.charAt(pos[0]) != ',') {
      dstOffset = -clock(s, pos, where, 24, 3)
    }
    if (pos[0] >= s.length()) {
      throw bad(where, "\"" + dstName + "\" has no transition rules, and POSIX leaves those" + " to the implementation")
    }
    if (s.charAt(pos[0]) != ',') {
      throw bad(where, "the rules do not begin with ','")
    }
    pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
    int[] start = rule(s, pos, where)
    if (pos[0] >= s.length() || s.charAt(pos[0]) != ',') {
      throw bad(where, "the second transition rule is missing")
    }
    pos[0] = pos[0] + 1
    int[] end = rule(s, pos, where)
    if (pos[0] != s.length()) {
      throw bad(where, "the string does not end after the second rule")
    }
    return new PosixTz(s, stdName, stdOffset, dstName, dstOffset, start, end)
  }

  // ---- the evaluation ----

  /* The day of a month the week-th weekday falls on, where week 5 means the last
     one. POSIX's Mm.w.d, and the reason a month is not a whole number of weeks:
     a fifth weekday exists in some months and not others, so "the last Sunday of
     March" is either the 4th or the 5th and has to be computed. */
  private static int weekDayOfMonth(int year, int month, int week, int day) {
    int firstWeekday = TimeMath.dayOfWeek(TimeMath.daysFromCivil(year, month, 1)).getValue() % 7
    int first = 1 + Math.floorMod(day - firstWeekday, 7)
    int out = first + (week - 1) * 7
    if (out > TimeMath.daysInMonth(year, month)) {
      out = out - 7
    }
    return out
  }

  // The local second of a rule in a year: the rule's date at midnight plus its
  // time, which may be negative or more than a day and so may leave the date the
  // rule names.
  private static long ruleSecond(int[] r, int year) {
    long firstOfYear = TimeMath.daysFromCivil(year, 1, 1)
    long epochDay
    if (r[0] == JULIAN_ONE) {
      epochDay = firstOfYear + (r[4] - 1)
      if (r[4] >= 60 && TimeMath.isLeapYear(year)) {
        epochDay = epochDay + 1
      }
    } else if (r[0] == DAY_OF_YEAR) {
      epochDay = firstOfYear + r[4]
    } else {
      epochDay = TimeMath.daysFromCivil(year, r[1], 1) + (weekDayOfMonth(year, r[1], r[2], r[3]) - 1)
    }
    return epochDay * 86400 + r[5]
  }

  // ---- what the callers use ----

  public String toString() {
    return text
  }

  public boolean hasDst() {
    return dstName != null
  }

  public int getStdOffset() {
    return stdOffset
  }

  public int getDstOffset() {
    return dstOffset
  }

  public String getName(boolean dst) {
    return dst ? dstName : stdName
  }

  /* The UT second at which the rule's start -- standard time to DST -- falls in
     a year, and the same for its end. The start's local time is in standard time
     because that is the offset in force before it, and the end's is in DST,
     which is POSIX's rule and the reason the two calls use different offsets. */
  public long startSecond(int year) {
    return ruleSecond(start, year) - stdOffset
  }

  public long endSecond(int year) {
    return ruleSecond(end, year) - dstOffset
  }

  public ZoneOffsetTransition startTransition(int year) {
    ZoneOffset from = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(stdOffset)
    ZoneOffset to = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(dstOffset)
    return new ZoneOffsetTransition(startSecond(year), from, to)
  }

  public ZoneOffsetTransition endTransition(int year) {
    ZoneOffset from = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(dstOffset)
    ZoneOffset to = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(stdOffset)
    return new ZoneOffsetTransition(endSecond(year), from, to)
  }

  /* The rule's answer for one UT second: DST, or standard time. The transitions
     of the year the second falls in, and of the years either side of it, are all
     computed, and the answer is the half that the latest of them at or before
     the second established. Asking the neighbours is what makes a rule with a
     negative or over-a-day transition time, and a rule whose DST is the winter
     (the southern hemisphere), fall out without a special case. */
  public boolean isDstAt(long second) {
    if (dstName == null) {
      return false
    }
    int year = TzMath.yearOf(second, stdOffset)
    boolean dst = false
    long latest = 0
    boolean found = false
    for (int y = year - 1 : y <= year + 1 : y++) {
      long s = startSecond(y)
      long e = endSecond(y)
      if (s <= second && (!found || s > latest)) {
        latest = s
        dst = true
        found = true
      }
      if (e <= second && (!found || e > latest)) {
        latest = e
        dst = false
        found = true
      }
    }
    return dst
  }

  public String nameAt(long second) {
    if (dstName == null) {
      return stdName
    }
    return isDstAt(second) ? dstName : stdName
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the file

/* One data block of a TZif file, as it was read: the transition times, the type
   each of them opens, and the type table itself with the designations resolved
   to strings. `end` is the offset in the file just past the block, which is what
   a version 2+ reader needs of the version 1 block -- its length -- and where
   the second block's end, and so the footer, is found. */
final class TzifBlock {
  public long[] times
  public int[] types
  public int[] offsets
  public boolean[] dst
  public String[] names
  public int end
}

/* A parsed TZif file: the version, the block that was read, and the footer rule
   if there is one. The parse is where every refusal about a malformed file
   lives, so the rest of this library can assume the numbers are consistent. */
final class TzifData {
  public final int version
  public final long[] transitions
  public final int[] transitionType
  public final int[] typeOffset
  public final boolean[] typeDst
  public final String[] typeName
  public final PosixTz rule

  private TzifData(int version, TzifBlock b, PosixTz rule) {
    this.version = version
    this.transitions = b.times
    this.transitionType = b.types
    this.typeOffset = b.offsets
    this.typeDst = b.dst
    this.typeName = b.names
    this.rule = rule
  }

  private static ZoneRulesException bad(String path, String why) {
    return new ZoneRulesException(path + " is not a TZif file this reader can use: " + why)
  }

  /* RFC 8536 stores every multi-octet number big-endian with all bits
     significant and two's complement for the signed ones, which is why these
     three are written by hand rather than read through a stream: this library's
     DataInputStream is java.io's and is big-endian too, but a DataInputStream of
     a byte[] would have to be built, and four shifts are cheaper than that. */
  private static int be32(byte[] b, int off) {
    return ((b[off] & 0xFF) << 24) | ((b[off + 1] & 0xFF) << 16) | ((b[off + 2] & 0xFF) << 8) | (b[off + 3] & 0xFF)
  }

  private static long be64(byte[] b, int off) {
    return ((long) be32(b, off) << 32) | (be32(b, off + 4) & 0xFFFFFFFFL)
  }

  /* The six counts of the header at an offset, in the order the format lists
     them: isutcnt, isstdcnt, leapcnt, timecnt, typecnt, charcnt. */
  private static int[] counts(byte[] b, String path, int at) {
    if ((long) at + 44L > b.length) {
      throw bad(path, "a header runs past the end of the file")
    }
    if (b[at] != 'T' || b[at + 1] != 'Z' || b[at + 2] != 'i' || b[at + 3] != 'f') {
      throw bad(path, "a header does not begin with the TZif magic")
    }
    int[] out = new int[6]
    for (int i = 0 : i < 6 : i++) {
      out[i] = be32(b, at + 20 + i * 4)
      if (out[i] < 0) {
        throw bad(path, "a header count overflows a signed 32 bit integer")
      }
    }
    if (out[4] == 0) {
      throw bad(path, "the type table is empty")
    }
    if (out[5] == 0) {
      throw bad(path, "the designations are empty")
    }
    return out
  }

  // The length of a data block, which is all a version 2+ reader needs of the
  // version 1 block: RFC 8536 asks a reader to calculate it and skip it rather
  // than read the same transitions twice at two widths.
  private static int blockLength(byte[] b, String path, int at, int timeSize) {
    int[] c = counts(b, path, at)
    long size = 44L + (long) c[3] * timeSize + c[3] + (long) c[4] * 6L + c[5] + (long) c[2] * (timeSize + 4L) + c[0] + c[1]
    if ((long) at + size > b.length) {
      throw bad(path, "a data block runs past the end of the file")
    }
    return (int) size
  }

  private static TzifBlock block(byte[] b, String path, int at, int timeSize) {
    int[] c = counts(b, path, at)
    int timecnt = c[3]
    int typecnt = c[4]
    int charcnt = c[5]
    int p = at + 44
    TzifBlock out = new TzifBlock()
    out.times = new long[timecnt]
    for (int i = 0 : i < timecnt : i++) {
      long t = timeSize == 4 ? (long) be32(b, p) : be64(b, p)
      if (i > 0 && t <= out.times[i - 1]) {
        throw bad(path, "the transition times are not strictly ascending")
      }
      out.times[i] = t
      p = p + timeSize
    }
    out.types = new int[timecnt]
    for (int i = 0 : i < timecnt : i++) {
      int t = b[p + i] & 0xFF
      if (t >= typecnt) {
        throw bad(path, "a transition names a local time type that does not exist")
      }
      out.types[i] = t
    }
    p = p + timecnt
    out.offsets = new int[typecnt]
    out.dst = new boolean[typecnt]
    out.names = new String[typecnt]
    int desigStart = p + typecnt * 6
    for (int i = 0 : i < typecnt : i++) {
      int utoff = be32(b, p + i * 6)
      int isdst = b[p + i * 6 + 4] & 0xFF
      int idx = b[p + i * 6 + 5] & 0xFF
      if (utoff < -64800 || utoff > 64800) {
        String why = " seconds is outside the +/-18:00 this library's ZoneOffset carries"
        throw bad(path, "a local time type offset of " + utoff + why)
      }
      if (isdst != 0 && isdst != 1) {
        throw bad(path, "the isdst octet of a local time type is " + isdst + ", which is neither 0 nor 1")
      }
      if (idx >= charcnt) {
        throw bad(path, "a local time type names a designation that does not exist")
      }
      int end = desigStart + idx
      while (end < desigStart + charcnt && b[end] != 0) {
        end = end + 1
      }
      if (end >= desigStart + charcnt) {
        throw bad(path, "a designation is not terminated by a NUL octet")
      }
      out.offsets[i] = utoff
      out.dst[i] = isdst == 1
      out.names[i] = Net.stringFrom(b, desigStart + idx, end - desigStart - idx)
    }
    /* The leap-second records and the two indicator series are stepped over.
       The indicators say how the transition times were *written* -- as UT, as
       standard time or as wall time -- which is a question a reader never asks.
       The leap records are refused, because a file that has them counts its
       times in UNIX leap seconds and ignoring the corrections would put every
       instant after 1972 out by the number of leap seconds. */
    if (c[2] != 0) {
      String why = " apply the corrections they describe"
      throw bad(path, "it carries " + c[2] + " leap-second records, and this reader does not" + why)
    }
    out.end = desigStart + charcnt + c[1] + c[0]
    return out
  }

  /* Read a file. The version octet decides which block is the file's data and
     whether there is a footer: a version 1 file is its first block alone, and a
     version 2, 3 or 4 file is its second block plus the footer, with the first
     block skipped by its calculated length. */
  public static TzifData parse(byte[] b, String path) {
    if (b.length < 44) {
      throw bad(path, "it is shorter than a header")
    }
    if (b[0] != 'T' || b[1] != 'Z' || b[2] != 'i' || b[3] != 'f') {
      throw bad(path, "its magic is not TZif")
    }
    byte v = b[4]
    if (v == 0) {
      return new TzifData(1, block(b, path, 0, 4), null)
    }
    if (v != '2' && v != '3' && v != '4') {
      throw bad(path, "its version octet is " + (int) v + ", which is not 0x00, '2', '3' or '4'")
    }
    int second = blockLength(b, path, 0, 4)
    TzifBlock b2 = block(b, path, second, 8)
    int at = b2.end
    if (at >= b.length || b[at] != '\n') {
      throw bad(path, "its footer does not begin with a newline")
    }
    int close = -1
    for (int i = at + 1 : i < b.length : i++) {
      if (b[i] == 0) {
        throw bad(path, "its footer holds a NUL octet")
      }
      if (b[i] == '\n') {
        close = i
        break
      }
    }
    if (close < 0) {
      throw bad(path, "its footer does not end with a newline")
    }
    if (close + 1 != b.length) {
      throw bad(path, "there are octets after its footer")
    }
    int version = v == '2' ? 2 : (v == '3' ? 3 : 4)
    if (close == at + 1) {
      // An empty TZ string: RFC 8536 says the information is not available, and
      // the block's own last transition is then the last word on the zone.
      return new TzifData(version, b2, null)
    }
    String tz = Net.stringFrom(b, at + 1, close - at - 1)
    return new TzifData(version, b2, PosixTz.parse(tz, path))
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the rules

/* java.time.zone.ZoneRules, over one parsed file: the offset, the standard
   offset, the DST flag, the abbreviation, and the transitions themselves.

   Per instant, the answer is a window. There is one window per transition (the
   transition opens it), one before the first transition, and, when the file has
   a footer, one open-ended window after the last transition that the footer rule
   decides -- and that last one is the reason the footer is parsed at all. The
   windows are precomputed at construction: the offset and the standard offset of
   each, the DST flag and the designation, so a lookup is a binary search and
   nothing more.

   The standard offset of a window is the offset of the most recent local time
   type that is not DST, which is Java's definition of it ("the offset that
   applies when daylight saving is not in operation") and what makes New York's
   standard offset -05:00 in July. The DST flag is the isdst octet of the type
   the file names, and for the footer window, which side of the rule the instant
   falls on -- the answer glibc's tm_isdst gives, and the answer a JVM gives
   except where its own copy of the database flattens negative DST (the note at
   the top of this file).

   One Java shape is deliberately not matched. Java's ZoneRules objects are
   shared between the ids that alias one zone (Europe/Kiev and Europe/Kyiv answer
   the same object, so getRules().equals is true), because its provider indexes
   its compiled database by the data. Here the rules are cached by id, and two
   ids that read the same file read it twice and hold two objects: the equals a
   Java program would get is identity, and identity of two ids' rules is not
   something this reader promises. */
class ZoneRules {
  // The window codes: the two that are not a transition index.
  private static final int BEFORE_FIRST = -1
  private static final int USE_RULE = -2

  // The offsets a local time could be in, one per distinct offset the file uses.
  // The candidate set of getValidOffsets, which then keeps only the ones the
  // zone really is in at the instant the local time would make.
  private static int[] distinctOffsets(TzifData data) {
    int[] out = new int[data.typeOffset.length + 2]
    int n = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < data.typeOffset.length : i++) {
      boolean seen = false
      for (int k = 0 : k < n : k++) {
        if (out[k] == data.typeOffset[i]) {
          seen = true
          break
        }
      }
      if (!seen) {
        out[n] = data.typeOffset[i]
        n = n + 1
      }
    }
    if (data.rule != null) {
      int[] more = new int[2]
      more[0] = data.rule.getStdOffset()
      more[1] = data.rule.hasDst() ? data.rule.getDstOffset() : data.rule.getStdOffset()
      for (int i = 0 : i < 2 : i++) {
        boolean seen = false
        for (int k = 0 : k < n : k++) {
          if (out[k] == more[i]) {
            seen = true
            break
          }
        }
        if (!seen) {
          out[n] = more[i]
          n = n + 1
        }
      }
    }
    int[] trimmed = new int[n]
    for (int i = 0 : i < n : i++) {
      trimmed[i] = out[i]
    }
    return trimmed
  }

  private final long[] transitions
  private final int[] afterOffset
  private final int[] afterStandard
  private final boolean[] afterDst
  private final String[] afterName
  private final int beforeOffset
  private final int beforeStandard
  private final boolean beforeDst
  private final String beforeName
  private final long[] localBefore
  private final long[] localAfter
  private final int[] distinct
  private final PosixTz rule
  private final ZoneOffset ruleStd
  private final ZoneOffset ruleDst

  private ZoneOffset[] cache
  private int cacheCount
  private List<ZoneOffsetTransition> history

  // ---- construction ----

  ZoneRules(TzifData data) {
    int n = data.transitions.length
    this.transitions = data.transitions
    this.rule = data.rule
    this.afterOffset = new int[n]
    this.afterStandard = new int[n]
    this.afterDst = new boolean[n]
    this.afterName = new String[n]
    this.localBefore = new long[n]
    this.localAfter = new long[n]
    /* The type in force before the first transition is the first type that is
       not DST, or type 0 when the table has no such type. RFC 8536 says type 0
       and tzfile(5) says the first non-DST one, and in every file of the
       database this was written against they are the same type -- but the second
       reading is the one the C library uses, so it is the one here. */
    int firstType = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < data.typeDst.length : i++) {
      if (!data.typeDst[i]) {
        firstType = i
        break
      }
    }
    this.beforeOffset = data.typeOffset[firstType]
    this.beforeDst = data.typeDst[firstType]
    this.beforeName = data.typeName[firstType]
    this.beforeStandard = data.typeOffset[firstType]
    int standard = this.beforeStandard
    int previous = firstType
    for (int i = 0 : i < n : i++) {
      int type = data.transitionType[i]
      this.afterOffset[i] = data.typeOffset[type]
      this.afterDst[i] = data.typeDst[type]
      this.afterName[i] = data.typeName[type]
      if (data.typeDst[type]) {
        this.afterStandard[i] = standard
      } else {
        standard = data.typeOffset[type]
        this.afterStandard[i] = standard
      }
      this.localBefore[i] = this.transitions[i] + data.typeOffset[previous]
      this.localAfter[i] = this.transitions[i] + this.afterOffset[i]
      previous = type
    }
    this.ruleStd = data.rule == null ? null : ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(data.rule.getStdOffset())
    this.ruleDst = data.rule == null || !data.rule.hasDst() ? null : ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(data.rule.getDstOffset())
    this.distinct = distinctOffsets(data)
    this.cache = new ZoneOffset[4]
    this.cacheCount = 0
  }

  /* A rule set that never moves: UTC, GMT, UT and every numeric offset id. The
     name is the zone id rather than the offset's own spelling of itself, because
     the abbreviation of the zone named UTC is "UTC" (which is what the file for
     it carries, and what glibc answers for TZ=UTC) while the id of its offset is
     "Z". */
  private ZoneRules(ZoneOffset offset, String name) {
    this.transitions = new long[0]
    this.afterOffset = new int[0]
    this.afterStandard = new int[0]
    this.afterDst = new boolean[0]
    this.afterName = new String[0]
    this.localBefore = new long[0]
    this.localAfter = new long[0]
    this.beforeOffset = offset.getTotalSeconds()
    this.beforeStandard = offset.getTotalSeconds()
    this.beforeDst = false
    this.beforeName = name
    this.distinct = new int[1]
    this.distinct[0] = offset.getTotalSeconds()
    this.rule = null
    this.ruleStd = null
    this.ruleDst = null
    this.cache = new ZoneOffset[1]
    this.cache[0] = offset
    this.cacheCount = 1
  }

  /* A rule set that is nothing but a TZ string's rule, for a TZ environment
     variable that is a rule rather than a zone id: no table, one open-ended
     window, and the rule's own transitions from then on. */
  private ZoneRules(PosixTz rule) {
    this.transitions = new long[0]
    this.afterOffset = new int[0]
    this.afterStandard = new int[0]
    this.afterDst = new boolean[0]
    this.afterName = new String[0]
    this.localBefore = new long[0]
    this.localAfter = new long[0]
    this.rule = rule
    this.ruleStd = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(rule.getStdOffset())
    this.ruleDst = rule.hasDst() ? ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(rule.getDstOffset()) : null
    this.beforeOffset = rule.getStdOffset()
    this.beforeStandard = rule.getStdOffset()
    this.beforeDst = false
    this.beforeName = rule.getName(false)
    int count = rule.hasDst() ? 2 : 1
    this.distinct = new int[count]
    this.distinct[0] = rule.getStdOffset()
    if (count == 2) {
      this.distinct[1] = rule.getDstOffset()
    }
    this.cache = new ZoneOffset[2]
    this.cacheCount = 0
  }

  public static ZoneRules fixed(ZoneOffset offset) {
    return new ZoneRules(offset, offset.getId())
  }

  public static ZoneRules ofNamedOffset(ZoneOffset offset, String name) {
    return new ZoneRules(offset, name)
  }

  public static ZoneRules fromPosix(PosixTz rule) {
    return new ZoneRules(rule)
  }

  // ---- the small pieces ----

  private ZoneOffset zone(int totalSeconds) {
    for (int i = 0 : i < cacheCount : i++) {
      if (cache[i].getTotalSeconds() == totalSeconds) {
        return cache[i]
      }
    }
    ZoneOffset out = ZoneOffset.ofTotalSeconds(totalSeconds)
    if (cacheCount == cache.length) {
      ZoneOffset[] bigger = new ZoneOffset[cache.length * 2]
      for (int i = 0 : i < cacheCount : i++) {
        bigger[i] = cache[i]
      }
      cache = bigger
    }
    cache[cacheCount] = out
    cacheCount = cacheCount + 1
    return out
  }

  /* Which window an instant falls in: BEFORE_FIRST before the first transition,
     USE_RULE at or after the last one when there is a footer, and otherwise the
     index of the transition that opened the window. */
  private int windowOf(long second) {
    int n = transitions.length
    if (n == 0) {
      return rule == null ? BEFORE_FIRST : USE_RULE
    }
    if (second < transitions[0]) {
      return BEFORE_FIRST
    }
    if (second >= transitions[n - 1]) {
      return rule == null ? n - 1 : USE_RULE
    }
    int lo = 0
    int hi = n - 1
    while (lo < hi) {
      int mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2
      if (transitions[mid] <= second) {
        lo = mid
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1
      }
    }
    return lo
  }

  private ZoneOffset offsetAt(long second) {
    int w = windowOf(second)
    if (w == USE_RULE) {
      return rule.isDstAt(second) ? ruleDst : ruleStd
    }
    if (w == BEFORE_FIRST) {
      return zone(beforeOffset)
    }
    return zone(afterOffset[w])
  }

  private ZoneOffsetTransition transitionAt(int i) {
    int before = i == 0 ? beforeOffset : afterOffset[i - 1]
    return new ZoneOffsetTransition(transitions[i], zone(before), zone(afterOffset[i]))
  }

  // The index of the last element of a non-decreasing array that is at most a
  // value, or -1 when there is none. A transition's two local times are monotone
  // in the transition index, so this is what finds the one whose local window a
  // local time falls in.
  private static int lastAtMost(long[] a, long v) {
    int lo = 0
    int hi = a.length - 1
    int out = -1
    while (lo <= hi) {
      int mid = (lo + hi) / 2
      if (a[mid] <= v) {
        out = mid
        lo = mid + 1
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1
      }
    }
    return out
  }

  private static int firstAbove(long[] a, long v) {
    for (int i = 0 : i < a.length : i++) {
      if (a[i] > v) {
        return i
      }
    }
    return -1
  }

  private static int lastBelow(long[] a, long v) {
    int out = -1
    for (int i = 0 : i < a.length : i++) {
      if (a[i] < v) {
        out = i
      }
    }
    return out
  }

  /* The rule's transition whose local window holds a local second, or null when
     none does: the same question getTransition asks of the table, asked of the
     rule, for a local time past the end of the table. */
  private ZoneOffsetTransition ruleTransitionFor(long local) {
    // A rule with no DST half (CST-8, <+01>-1) makes no transitions at all, so
    // no local time can be in a gap or an overlap that one of them left.
    if (rule == null || !rule.hasDst()) {
      return null
    }
    int year = TzMath.yearOf(local, -rule.getStdOffset())
    for (int y = year - 1 : y <= year + 1 : y++) {
      ZoneOffsetTransition start = rule.startTransition(y)
      long startBefore = start.toEpochSecond() + start.getOffsetBefore().getTotalSeconds()
      long startAfter = start.toEpochSecond() + start.getOffsetAfter().getTotalSeconds()
      if (local >= Math.min(startBefore, startAfter) && local < Math.max(startBefore, startAfter)) {
        return start
      }
      if (rule.hasDst()) {
        ZoneOffsetTransition end = rule.endTransition(y)
        long endBefore = end.toEpochSecond() + end.getOffsetBefore().getTotalSeconds()
        long endAfter = end.toEpochSecond() + end.getOffsetAfter().getTotalSeconds()
        if (local >= Math.min(endBefore, endAfter) && local < Math.max(endBefore, endAfter)) {
          return end
        }
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  private ZoneOffsetTransition firstRuleTransitionAfter(long t) {
    int year = TzMath.yearOf(t, offsetAt(t).getTotalSeconds())
    for (int y = year - 1 : y <= year + 2 : y++) {
      ZoneOffsetTransition best = null
      ZoneOffsetTransition start = rule.startTransition(y)
      if (start.toEpochSecond() > t) {
        best = start
      }
      if (rule.hasDst()) {
        ZoneOffsetTransition end = rule.endTransition(y)
        if (end.toEpochSecond() > t && (best == null || end.toEpochSecond() < best.toEpochSecond())) {
          best = end
        }
      }
      if (best != null) {
        return best
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  private ZoneOffsetTransition lastRuleTransitionBefore(long t) {
    int year = TzMath.yearOf(t, offsetAt(t).getTotalSeconds())
    for (int y = year + 1 : y >= year - 2 : y--) {
      ZoneOffsetTransition best = null
      if (rule.hasDst()) {
        ZoneOffsetTransition end = rule.endTransition(y)
        if (end.toEpochSecond() < t) {
          best = end
        }
      }
      ZoneOffsetTransition start = rule.startTransition(y)
      if (start.toEpochSecond() < t && (best == null || start.toEpochSecond() > best.toEpochSecond())) {
        best = start
      }
      if (best != null) {
        return best
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  // ---- what an instant asks ----

  public ZoneOffset getOffset(Instant instant) {
    return offsetAt(instant.getEpochSecond())
  }

  public ZoneOffset getStandardOffset(Instant instant) {
    long second = instant.getEpochSecond()
    int w = windowOf(second)
    if (w == USE_RULE) {
      return ruleStd
    }
    if (w == BEFORE_FIRST) {
      return zone(beforeStandard)
    }
    return zone(afterStandard[w])
  }

  public boolean isDaylightSavings(Instant instant) {
    long second = instant.getEpochSecond()
    int w = windowOf(second)
    if (w == USE_RULE) {
      return rule.hasDst() && rule.isDstAt(second)
    }
    if (w == BEFORE_FIRST) {
      return beforeDst
    }
    return afterDst[w]
  }

  /* The zone's abbreviation for an instant: the designation the file names for
     the type in force, or the name on the rule's side. java.time has no such
     method -- a JVM reaches an abbreviation through a formatter, which answers a
     locale's name for the zone rather than the file's designation -- so this is
     this library's own spelling of the thing the data carries. */
  public String getAbbreviation(Instant instant) {
    long second = instant.getEpochSecond()
    int w = windowOf(second)
    if (w == USE_RULE) {
      return rule.nameAt(second)
    }
    if (w == BEFORE_FIRST) {
      return beforeName
    }
    return afterName[w]
  }

  public boolean isFixedOffset() {
    if (transitions.length == 0) {
      return rule == null || !rule.hasDst()
    }
    return false
  }

  // ---- what a local date-time asks ----

  /* The offsets a local date-time can be in: none, one, or two. A local time
     inside a spring-forward gap is in none of them, which is why
     ZonedDateTime.of has to move it; a local time inside a fall-back overlap is
     in two, and they come back in the order of the transition -- the one in
     force before it first -- which is Java's order and the one
     ZonedDateTime.of uses to pick the earlier. */
  public List<ZoneOffset> getValidOffsets(LocalDateTime dt) {
    long local = TzMath.localSecond(dt)
    List<ZoneOffset> out = new ArrayList<ZoneOffset>()
    for (int i = 0 : i < distinct.length : i++) {
      ZoneOffset offset = zone(distinct[i])
      if (offsetAt(local - distinct[i]).equals(offset)) {
        out.add(offset)
      }
    }
    if (out.size() == 2 && out.get(0).getTotalSeconds() < out.get(1).getTotalSeconds()) {
      ZoneOffset first = out.get(0)
      out.set(0, out.get(1))
      out.set(1, first)
    }
    return out
  }

  /* The offset of a local date-time, which for a gap or an overlap is the one in
     force *before* the transition: Java's answer, and the reason a local time in
     a gap maps to the offset it would have had rather than the one it will. */
  public ZoneOffset getOffset(LocalDateTime dt) {
    List<ZoneOffset> valid = getValidOffsets(dt)
    if (valid.size() == 1) {
      return valid.get(0)
    }
    ZoneOffsetTransition trans = getTransition(dt)
    if (trans != null) {
      return trans.getOffsetBefore()
    }
    // Unreachable for any file this reader accepts: a local time is in no offset
    // or in two only because a transition put it there, and that transition is
    // what getTransition found. Refused rather than guessed if it ever happens.
    throw new ZoneRulesException("no offset applies to " + dt + " and no transition explains it")
  }

  /* The transition a local date-time falls in, or null when it falls in none.
     The local window of a transition is between the local time just before it and
     the local time just after: empty for a change of a second or less, a gap when
     the clocks went forward, an overlap when they went back. */
  public ZoneOffsetTransition getTransition(LocalDateTime dt) {
    long local = TzMath.localSecond(dt)
    int n = transitions.length
    if (n > 0) {
      int before = lastAtMost(localBefore, local)
      int after = lastAtMost(localAfter, local)
      if (before != after) {
        return transitionAt(Math.max(before, after))
      }
      if (local < Math.max(localBefore[n - 1], localAfter[n - 1])) {
        return null
      }
    }
    return ruleTransitionFor(local)
  }

  /* The table's transitions, oldest first: what Java answers, and the same list
     of *this* reader's transitions. Java's list is unmodifiable and this one is
     the rules' own, which a caller could mutate -- the alternative is a copy on
     every call for a list that is usually asked for once in a test. */
  public List<ZoneOffsetTransition> getTransitions() {
    if (history == null) {
      List<ZoneOffsetTransition> out = new ArrayList<ZoneOffsetTransition>()
      for (int i = 0 : i < transitions.length : i++) {
        out.add(transitionAt(i))
      }
      history = out
    }
    return history
  }

  /* The next transition strictly after an instant, which past the end of the
     table is one the footer rule will make: Java answers those too, from the
     rules it derives the same way, so a program can ask what the clocks do next
     year as well as what they did in 1979. */
  public ZoneOffsetTransition nextTransition(Instant instant) {
    long t = instant.getEpochSecond()
    int n = transitions.length
    if (n > 0 && t < transitions[n - 1]) {
      int i = firstAbove(transitions, t)
      return i < 0 ? null : transitionAt(i)
    }
    if (rule == null || !rule.hasDst()) {
      return null
    }
    return firstRuleTransitionAfter(t)
  }

  public ZoneOffsetTransition previousTransition(Instant instant) {
    long t = instant.getEpochSecond()
    if (instant.getNano() > 0) {
      // Java's rule: an instant inside a second counts as the next second for
      // this question, so a transition exactly at the start of it is not "the
      // previous" one.
      t = t + 1
    }
    int n = transitions.length
    if (n > 0 && (t <= transitions[n - 1] || rule == null || !rule.hasDst())) {
      int i = lastBelow(transitions, t)
      return i < 0 ? null : transitionAt(i)
    }
    if (rule == null || !rule.hasDst()) {
      return null
    }
    return lastRuleTransitionBefore(t)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the zoned date

/* java.time.ZonedDateTime: a local date and time with the offset it is at and
   the zone it is in.

   The three fields are the whole of it, and the interesting part is what happens
   when a local time does not determine the offset. A local time in a gap -- 02:30
   on the morning New York springs forward -- is moved forward by the length of
   the gap and takes the offset after it, so 2021-03-14T02:30 in New York is
   03:30-04:00; a local time in an overlap -- 01:30 on the morning it falls back
   -- keeps the offset in force *before* the transition, so 2021-11-07T01:30 is
   01:30-04:00, the first of the two times that wall clock says. Both are Java's
   rules, and both show up in what a program prints rather than in the
   arithmetic, which is why the tests pin them.

   The arithmetic is Java's split, and the split is the point. Adding days,
   months or years is calendar arithmetic on the local date and time, and then the
   offset is resolved again against the *old* offset preferred: a day later at
   12:00 is 12:00 the next day even when that day is 23 hours long, and a day
   later at 01:30 inside a fall-back overlap is still 01:30-04:00, because the
   offset it was at is one of the two that are still valid there. Adding hours,
   minutes, seconds or milliseconds is arithmetic on the instant, and the answer
   is the local time the zone shows at the new instant: 24 hours after noon on the
   day before a spring forward is 13:00, not 12:00, which is the difference
   between a day and a day's worth of seconds.

   What this class does not have, and why, in the shape this library already has.
   No ofStrict/ofLenient (they exist in Java to refuse and to repair a local time
   that does not resolve, and of() here always resolves the way Java's lenient
   path does). No ChronoUnit or ChronoField accessors, no TemporalAdjuster, no
   formatter: those are java.time's general machinery, and this library does not
   have it. And no plusWeeks, minusWeeks, plusNanos or minusNanos, because
   lib/20_time.teyru does not have them either -- its LocalDate's arithmetic is
   days, months and years and its LocalDateTime's is days, hours, minutes and
   seconds, so a zoned date here has the same set plus the two units (hours and
   below) that the zone itself needs to get right. What it *does* have that Java
   also has is withEarlierOffsetAtOverlap and withLaterOffsetAtOverlap, which are
   the only public way to choose between the two offsets of an overlap.

   One precision note, because toString and toInstant do not agree for a value
   below a millisecond: this library's Instant is a System.currentTimeMillis and
   cannot carry more than milliseconds, so a zoned date built from a local time
   with microseconds loses them in toInstant(), while its toString prints them.
   Java's Instant carries nanoseconds and has no such seam. */
class ZonedDateTime implements Comparable<ZonedDateTime> {
  private final LocalDateTime dateTime
  private final ZoneOffset offset
  private final ZoneId zone

  private ZonedDateTime(LocalDateTime dateTime, ZoneOffset offset, ZoneId zone) {
    this.dateTime = dateTime
    this.offset = offset
    this.zone = zone
  }

  /* Java's ofLocal: a local date and time is placed in a zone, and the offset
     comes from the valid offsets, from the gap (the local time is moved through
     it), or from the overlap (the preferred offset when it is one of the two, the
     earlier one otherwise). */
  private static ZonedDateTime ofLocal(LocalDateTime dateTime, ZoneId zone, ZoneOffset preferred) {
    if (zone instanceof ZoneOffset) {
      return new ZonedDateTime(dateTime, (ZoneOffset) zone, zone)
    }
    ZoneRules rules = zone.getRules()
    List<ZoneOffset> valid = rules.getValidOffsets(dateTime)
    ZoneOffset offset
    if (valid.size() == 1) {
      offset = valid.get(0)
    } else if (valid.size() == 0) {
      ZoneOffsetTransition trans = rules.getTransition(dateTime)
      dateTime = TzMath.plusSeconds(dateTime, trans.getDuration().getSeconds())
      offset = trans.getOffsetAfter()
    } else {
      if (preferred != null && valid.contains(preferred)) {
        offset = preferred
      } else {
        offset = valid.get(0)
      }
    }
    return new ZonedDateTime(dateTime, offset, zone)
  }

  /* Java's create: an instant is placed in a zone. The offset is looked up at
     the second -- a TZif transition is a whole second, so the fraction cannot
     change which offset applies -- and the local time is that second plus it. */
  private static ZonedDateTime create(long second, int nanos, ZoneId zone) {
    ZoneOffset offset = zone.getRules().getOffset(Instant.ofEpochSecond(second))
    return new ZonedDateTime(TzMath.localDateTime(second, nanos, offset.getTotalSeconds()), offset, zone)
  }

  private long toEpochSecond() {
    return TzMath.localSecond(dateTime) - offset.getTotalSeconds()
  }

  private ZonedDateTime resolveLocal(LocalDateTime dt) {
    return ofLocal(dt, zone, offset)
  }

  // ---- the factories ----

  public static ZonedDateTime of(LocalDateTime dateTime, ZoneId zone) {
    if (zone == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("zone")
    }
    if (dateTime == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("localDateTime")
    }
    return ofLocal(dateTime, zone, null)
  }

  public static ZonedDateTime of(LocalDate date, LocalTime time, ZoneId zone) {
    return of(LocalDateTime.of(date, time), zone)
  }

  public static ZonedDateTime of(int year, int month, int day, int hour, int minute, int second, int nanoOfSecond, ZoneId zone) {
    return of(LocalDateTime.of(LocalDate.of(year, month, day), LocalTime.of(hour, minute, second, nanoOfSecond)), zone)
  }

  public static ZonedDateTime ofInstant(Instant instant, ZoneId zone) {
    if (zone == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("zone")
    }
    return create(instant.getEpochSecond(), instant.getNano(), zone)
  }

  /* now and now(zone) read the clock -- one instant, shown in the zone asked
     for: the default zone for now(), which is this class's one use of it, and the
     zone given for now(zone). */
  public static ZonedDateTime now() {
    return ofInstant(Instant.now(), ZoneId.systemDefault())
  }

  public static ZonedDateTime now(ZoneId zone) {
    return ofInstant(Instant.now(), zone)
  }

  // ---- the parts ----

  public LocalDateTime toLocalDateTime() {
    return dateTime
  }

  public LocalDate toLocalDate() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate()
  }

  public LocalTime toLocalTime() {
    return dateTime.toLocalTime()
  }

  public ZoneOffset getOffset() {
    return offset
  }

  public ZoneId getZone() {
    return zone
  }

  public int getYear() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate().getYear()
  }

  public int getMonthValue() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate().getMonthValue()
  }

  public Month getMonth() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate().getMonth()
  }

  public int getDayOfMonth() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate().getDayOfMonth()
  }

  public DayOfWeek getDayOfWeek() {
    return dateTime.toLocalDate().getDayOfWeek()
  }

  public int getHour() {
    return dateTime.toLocalTime().getHour()
  }

  public int getMinute() {
    return dateTime.toLocalTime().getMinute()
  }

  public int getSecond() {
    return dateTime.toLocalTime().getSecond()
  }

  public int getNano() {
    return dateTime.toLocalTime().getNano()
  }

  /* The instant this zoned date is at, to the millisecond: every Instant in this
     library is a System.currentTimeMillis, so a local time carrying microseconds
     or nanoseconds loses the part below the millisecond here. Java keeps it; this
     library's Instant cannot hold it, and saying so is better than a toString
     that prints a fraction toInstant does not return. */
  public Instant toInstant() {
    return Instant.ofEpochMilli(toEpochSecond() * 1000L + dateTime.toLocalTime().getNano() / 1000000)
  }

  // ---- the arithmetic ----

  public ZonedDateTime plusDays(long days) {
    return resolveLocal(dateTime.plusDays(days))
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusMonths(long months) {
    return resolveLocal(LocalDateTime.of(dateTime.toLocalDate().plusMonths(months), dateTime.toLocalTime()))
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusYears(long years) {
    return resolveLocal(LocalDateTime.of(dateTime.toLocalDate().plusYears(years), dateTime.toLocalTime()))
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusHours(long hours) {
    return create(toEpochSecond() + hours * 3600L, getNano(), zone)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusMinutes(long minutes) {
    return create(toEpochSecond() + minutes * 60L, getNano(), zone)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusSeconds(long seconds) {
    return create(toEpochSecond() + seconds, getNano(), zone)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime plusMillis(long millis) {
    long nanos = (long) getNano() + millis * 1000000L
    return create(toEpochSecond() + Math.floorDiv(nanos, 1000000000L), (int) Math.floorMod(nanos, 1000000000L), zone)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusDays(long days) {
    return plusDays(-days)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusMonths(long months) {
    return plusMonths(-months)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusYears(long years) {
    return plusYears(-years)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusHours(long hours) {
    return plusHours(-hours)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusMinutes(long minutes) {
    return plusMinutes(-minutes)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusSeconds(long seconds) {
    return plusSeconds(-seconds)
  }

  public ZonedDateTime minusMillis(long millis) {
    return plusMillis(-millis)
  }

  // ---- the zone ----

  /* The same instant, shown in another zone. The instant does not move, so the
     local time and the offset both do. */
  public ZonedDateTime withZoneSameInstant(ZoneId newZone) {
    if (newZone == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("zone")
    }
    return create(toEpochSecond(), getNano(), newZone)
  }

  /* The two ways to choose between the offsets of an overlap, which is the one
     case where a zoned date's local time and its instant do not settle which
     offset it carries: 01:30 on the morning New York falls back is at -04:00 and
     at -05:00, and of() takes the earlier one. These move to the other one, and
     do nothing at all when the local time is not in an overlap. */
  public ZonedDateTime withEarlierOffsetAtOverlap() {
    ZoneOffsetTransition trans = zone.getRules().getTransition(dateTime)
    if (trans != null && trans.isOverlap() && !trans.getOffsetBefore().equals(offset)) {
      return new ZonedDateTime(dateTime, trans.getOffsetBefore(), zone)
    }
    return this
  }

  public ZonedDateTime withLaterOffsetAtOverlap() {
    ZoneOffsetTransition trans = zone.getRules().getTransition(dateTime)
    if (trans != null && trans.isOverlap() && !trans.getOffsetAfter().equals(offset)) {
      return new ZonedDateTime(dateTime, trans.getOffsetAfter(), zone)
    }
    return this
  }

  /* The same local date and time, read in another zone: the wall clock does not
     move, the instant does. */
  public ZonedDateTime withZoneSameLocal(ZoneId newZone) {
    if (newZone == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("zone")
    }
    return ofLocal(dateTime, newZone, offset)
  }

  // ---- the comparisons ----

  /* isBefore and isAfter compare the instants, not the local times: 03:30 in New
     York is before 03:30 at -05:00, because it is an hour earlier in the world. */
  public boolean isBefore(ZonedDateTime other) {
    long a = toEpochSecond()
    long b = other.toEpochSecond()
    if (a != b) {
      return a < b
    }
    return getNano() < other.getNano()
  }

  public boolean isAfter(ZonedDateTime other) {
    long a = toEpochSecond()
    long b = other.toEpochSecond()
    if (a != b) {
      return a > b
    }
    return getNano() > other.getNano()
  }

  public boolean isEqual(ZonedDateTime other) {
    return toEpochSecond() == other.toEpochSecond() && getNano() == other.getNano()
  }

  /* equals is the local date and time, the offset and the zone: the same instant
     in two zones is not equal, which is Java's rule and the reason compareTo and
     equals disagree for zoned values. compareTo compares the instant first, then
     the local date and time, then the zone id -- Java's order, so a sorted list
     of zoned dates is sorted by the moment each is at. */
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
      return true
    }
    if (o instanceof ZonedDateTime == false) {
      return false
    }
    ZonedDateTime other = (ZonedDateTime) o
    return dateTime.equals(other.dateTime) && offset.equals(other.offset) && zone.equals(other.zone)
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return dateTime.hashCode() ^ offset.hashCode() ^ Integer.rotateLeft(zone.hashCode(), 3)
  }

  public int compareTo(ZonedDateTime other) {
    long a = toEpochSecond()
    long b = other.toEpochSecond()
    if (a < b) {
      return -1
    }
    if (a > b) {
      return 1
    }
    if (getNano() < other.getNano()) {
      return -1
    }
    if (getNano() > other.getNano()) {
      return 1
    }
    int c = dateTime.compareTo(other.dateTime)
    if (c != 0) {
      return c
    }
    return zone.getId().compareTo(other.zone.getId())
  }

  /* Java's toString: the local date and time, the offset, and -- unless the zone
     *is* the offset -- the zone's id in brackets. That last test is what makes
     "2021-03-14T07:30Z" from a date in ZoneOffset.UTC and
     "2021-03-14T07:30Z[UTC]" from the same instant in the zone named UTC. */
  public String toString() {
    String out = dateTime.toString() + offset.toString()
    if (zone instanceof ZoneOffset == false) {
      out = out + "[" + zone.getId() + "]"
    }
    return out
  }
}
